# `stopPropagation` and `preventDefault`

[Last lesson](<09 - Event Bubbling and Propagation.webnb>) we saw how an event travels: down through the capture phase to the target, then back up through the bubbling phase. This lesson is about two methods that let us interfere with that.

Both are methods on the **event object**, the one passed into our callback.

## `event.stopPropagation()`

`stopPropagation` **prevents the event from advancing any further**. Whatever stage it is in, it stops there, and no listener further along the route runs at all.

Here is the six-listener setup from last lesson, with one addition: the button's bubbling listener stops the event.

```html id=nestboxes2
<div id="outer">Outer
  <div id="inner">Inner
    <button>Click me</button>
  </div>
</div>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const outer = document.querySelector('#outer');
const inner = document.querySelector('#inner');
const button = document.querySelector('button');

outer.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('outer capture'), true);
inner.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('inner capture'), true);
button.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('button capture'), true);

button.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  console.log('button bubbling');
  event.stopPropagation();
});

inner.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('inner bubbling'));
outer.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('outer bubbling'));
```
```+id=nestboxes2
```
```+css
#outer { background-color: #aaa; padding: 20px; }
#inner { background-color: #ccc; padding: 20px; }
```

Click the button. The first four listeners run, and then the event stops: `inner bubbling` and `outer bubbling` never happen.

It works in the capture phase too, and there it is more dramatic, because the event never even reaches the target:

```{javascript autorun}
const outer = document.querySelector('#outer');
const inner = document.querySelector('#inner');
const button = document.querySelector('button');

outer.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('outer capture'), true);

inner.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  console.log('inner capture');
  event.stopPropagation();
}, true);

button.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('button capture'), true);
button.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('button bubbling'));
inner.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('inner bubbling'));
outer.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('outer bubbling'));
```
```+id=nestboxes2
```
```+css
#outer { background-color: #aaa; padding: 20px; }
#inner { background-color: #ccc; padding: 20px; }
```

Only `outer capture` and `inner capture` run. The button was clicked, and the button's own listeners never fired, because the event was stopped on the way down.

Note that the order in which the listeners were *added* makes no difference to any of this. Only the phase and the position in the tree matter.

## `event.preventDefault()`

`preventDefault` does something completely different: it **prevents the browser's built-in behavior** for that interaction.

Plenty of elements do something on their own when you interact with them, with no JavaScript involved:

- Clicking a link navigates to its `href`.
- Clicking a checkbox toggles it.
- Submitting a form reloads the page with the form's data.
- Pressing Enter in a form submits it.

`preventDefault` cancels that.

```html id=page1
<p><a href="https://mgoblue.com/">Michigan Athletics</a></p>
<p><input type="checkbox"> Try to check me</p>
<p id="log">Try the link and the checkbox.</p>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const link = document.querySelector('a');
const box = document.querySelector('input');
const log = document.querySelector('#log');

link.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  log.textContent = 'the link did not navigate';
});

box.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  log.textContent = 'the checkbox did not toggle';
});
```
```+id=page1
```

Click the link: the callback runs, and the browser stays put. Click the checkbox: the callback runs, and the box refuses to tick.

## They Are Independent

This is the distinction to hold on to, and the two names do not help:

- **`stopPropagation`** controls **where the event goes**. It says nothing about default behavior.
- **`preventDefault`** controls **what the browser does**. It says nothing about propagation.

Calling `preventDefault` does *not* stop the event traveling: ancestors' listeners still run. And calling `stopPropagation` does *not* stop the browser doing its usual thing: a link with `stopPropagation` and no `preventDefault` still navigates.

If you want both, call both.

```html id=page2
<div id="wrap" style="background-color: #eee; padding: 16px;">
  <a href="https://mgoblue.com/">Michigan Athletics</a>
</div>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const outer = document.querySelector('#wrap');
const link = document.querySelector('a');

outer.addEventListener('click', () => {
  console.log('the wrapper still heard the click');
});

link.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  console.log('default prevented, but the event carries on');
});
```
```+id=page2
```

Click the link. The page does not navigate, and the wrapper's listener runs anyway.

## Exercises

1. Stop the button's click from reaching its parent, so that clicking logs `child` and nothing else.

    ```html id=page3
    <div id="parent" style="background-color: #eee; padding: 16px;">
      <button id="child">Click</button>
    </div>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const parent = document.querySelector('#parent');
    const button = document.querySelector('#child');

    parent.addEventListener('click', () => {
      console.log('parent');
    });

    button.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      // Write your code here
      console.log('child');
    });
    ```
    ```+id=page3
    ```
    ```+solution
    const parent = document.querySelector('#parent');
    const button = document.querySelector('#child');

    parent.addEventListener('click', () => {
      console.log('parent');
    });

    button.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      event.stopPropagation();
      console.log('child');
    });
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#child').click().run('Could not find the `#child` button', 'Clicked the button');
    check.consoleLogged('child').run('The button\'s own listener should still run', 'The child listener ran');
    wrap(console.didLog('parent')).should('equal', false).run('The parent listener should not run. Call `event.stopPropagation()` in the button\'s listener', 'Stopped the event from bubbling');
    ```

2. Stop the event during the **capture** phase, on the parent, so that the button's own listener never runs at all.

    ```{javascript}
    const parent = document.querySelector('#parent');
    const button = document.querySelector('#child');

    // Write your capture listener here

    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
      console.log('button');
    });
    ```
    ```+id=page3
    ```
    ```+solution
    const parent = document.querySelector('#parent');
    const button = document.querySelector('#child');

    parent.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      console.log('stopped on the way down');
      event.stopPropagation();
    }, true);

    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
      console.log('button');
    });
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#child').click().run('Could not find the `#child` button', 'Clicked the button');
    wrap(console.didLog('button')).should('equal', false).run('The button\'s listener should never run. Add a listener on the parent with `true` as the third argument, and call `event.stopPropagation()` in it', 'The event was stopped before it reached the button');
    wrap(console.history().length > 0).should('equal', true).run('Log something from your capture listener so you can see it ran', 'The capture listener ran');
    ```

3. Stop the checkbox from ticking when it is clicked, while still logging `blocked`.

    ```html id=page4
    <input id="agree" type="checkbox"> <label for="agree">I agree</label>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const box = document.querySelector('#agree');

    box.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      // Write your code here
      console.log('blocked');
    });
    ```
    ```+id=page4
    ```
    ```+solution
    const box = document.querySelector('#agree');

    box.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      event.preventDefault();
      console.log('blocked');
    });
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(box.checked).should('equal', false).run('The checkbox should start unchecked', 'The checkbox starts unchecked');
    assert('#agree').click().run('Could not find the `#agree` checkbox', 'Clicked the checkbox');
    check.consoleLogged('blocked').run('The listener should still log `blocked`', 'The listener ran');
    wrap(box.checked).should('equal', false).run('The checkbox should still be unchecked. Call `event.preventDefault()` to cancel the browser\'s built-in toggling', 'The default toggle was prevented');
    ```

